## Core JavaScript/TypeScript Skills

### 1. Modern JavaScript Features

**ES6+ Syntax:**
Modern JavaScript (ES6+) provides cleaner, more expressive syntax. Understanding these features is crucial as they're used extensively in Node.js development.

**Key Features:**
- **Arrow functions** - Concise syntax, lexical `this` binding
- **Destructuring** - Extract values from objects/arrays easily
- **Spread operator** - Expand arrays/objects, combine them
- **Template literals** - String interpolation with backticks
- **Classes** - Object-oriented programming patterns
- **Modules** - Code organization and reusability

**Arrow Functions Explanation:**
Arrow functions provide a concise syntax for writing functions. They automatically bind `this` to the enclosing scope (lexical binding), which prevents common errors with `this` context.

```javascript
// Traditional function
function add(a, b) {
  return a + b;
}

// Arrow function (equivalent)
const add = (a, b) => a + b;


// Why use arrow functions:
// 1. Concise syntax
// 2. Lexical `this` binding (no need for .bind() in callbacks)
// 3. Can't be used as constructors (prevents common mistakes)

const users = [
  { name: 'John', age: 30 },
  { name: 'Jane', age: 25 }
];

// Arrow functions perfect for array methods
users.map(user => ({ name: user.name, senior: user.age > 28 }));

// Destructuring - Extract values from objects/arrays
// This makes code more readable and reduces variable declarations
const user = { name: 'John', email: 'john@example.com', age: 30 };
const { name, email } = user; // Extract name and email
// name = 'John', email = 'john@example.com'

// Default values in destructuring
const { phone = 'N/A' } = user; // phone will be 'N/A' if not present

// Array destructuring
const numbers = [1, 2, 3, 4, 5];
const [first, second, ...rest] = numbers;
// first = 1, second = 2, rest = [3, 4, 5]

// Template literals - String interpolation
// Replaces concatenation: 'Hello ' + name + ', you have ' + count + ' messages'
const message = `Hello ${name}, you have ${count} messages`;
// Much more readable, especially with complex expressions
const formatted = `User: ${user.name.toUpperCase()}, Active: ${user.active ? 'Yes' : 'No'}`;

// Classes - Object-oriented syntax (syntactic sugar over prototypes)
class User {
  constructor(name, email) {
    this.name = name;
    this.email = email;
  }
  
  // Instance method
  greet() {
    return `Hello ${this.name}`;
  }
  
  // Static method (called on class, not instance)
  static fromJSON(json) {
    return new User(json.name, json.email);
  }
}

// Usage
const john = new User('John', 'john@example.com');
console.log(john.greet()); // "Hello John"

// Inheritance
class Admin extends User {
  constructor(name, email, role) {
    super(name, email); // Call parent constructor
    this.role = role;
  }
  
  // Override method
  greet() {
    return `Hello ${this.name}, you are an ${this.role}`;
  }
}

// Spread operator - Expand arrays/objects
// Array spreading: concatenate or copy arrays
const arr1 = [1, 2, 3];
const arr2 = [4, 5, 6];
const combined = [...arr1, ...arr2]; // [1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6]

// Object spreading: merge objects or copy with modifications
const obj1 = { name: 'John', age: 30 };
const obj2 = { email: 'john@example.com' };
const merged = { ...obj1, ...obj2 }; // { name: 'John', age: 30, email: 'john@example.com' }

// Useful for creating copies without mutations (important in React/immutable patterns)
const updated = { ...user, age: 31 }; // Create new object with modified age
console.log(user); // Original unchanged
```

**Theory Notes:**
- Arrow functions use "lexical this" - `this` is determined by the surrounding scope, not the call context
- Destructuring reduces nested property access and makes intent clear
- Template literals support multi-line strings and expressions
- Classes make inheritance patterns clearer than prototype chains
- Spread operator enables functional/immutable patterns

### 2. TypeScript Mastery

**Type System:**
- Interfaces, types, generics, unions
- Enums, decorators, utility types
- Strict mode configuration

**Type System Explanation:**
TypeScript adds static type checking to JavaScript, catching errors at compile-time rather than runtime. This is crucial for large applications where JavaScript's dynamic typing can lead to subtle bugs.

**Key Benefits of TypeScript:**
- Type safety - Catch errors before runtime
- Better IDE autocomplete and documentation
- Self-documenting code - Types serve as documentation
- Refactoring safety - Change types, compiler tells you what breaks
- Better team communication - Types clarify intent

```typescript
// Interfaces - Define contract for object shape
// Use for object types that will be implemented by classes
interface User {
  id: number;
  name: string;
  email?: string; // Optional property (? makes it optional)
}

// Type - More flexible, can be primitives, unions, tuples
type Status = 'active' | 'inactive' | 'pending'; // Union type
type ID = string | number; // Can be string or number
type Response = { data: User } | { error: string }; // Can be one of two shapes

// Generics - Reusable types with type parameters
// Allows writing generic functions/classes that work with multiple types
function getValue<T>(value: T): T {
  return value;
}

getValue<string>('hello'); // T is string
getValue<number>(42); // T is number

// Generic interface for API responses
interface ApiResponse<T> {
  status: number;
  data: T;
  error?: string;
}

// Reuse for different data types
const userResponse: ApiResponse<User> = {
  status: 200,
  data: { id: 1, name: 'John' }
};

const usersResponse: ApiResponse<User[]> = {
  status: 200,
  data: [{ id: 1, name: 'John' }]
};

// Utility Types - Built-in TypeScript types for common patterns
type ReadonlyUser = Readonly<User>; // All properties become readonly
type UserPreview = Omit<User, 'email'>; // Exclude email property
type PartialUser = Partial<User>; // All properties become optional
type UserRequired = Required<User>; // All properties become required

// Example usage
const preview: UserPreview = { id: 1, name: 'John' }; // email is not allowed

// Decorators - Add metadata or modify class/method behavior
// Commonly used in frameworks like NestJS, Angular
@Controller('/users')
class UserController {
  @Get('/:id')
  getUser(id: string) { }
  
  @Post()
  createUser(body: User) { }
}

// Enums - Define a set of named constants
enum Role {
  ADMIN = 'admin',
  USER = 'user',
  GUEST = 'guest'
}

const userRole: Role = Role.ADMIN;
```

**Theory Notes:**
- Use `interface` for object contracts, `type` for more complex shapes (unions, tuples)
- Generics enable reusable, type-safe code
- Utility types prevent code duplication for common type transformations
- Strict mode (`"strict": true` in tsconfig) catches most TypeScript errors
- Decorators are stage 3 proposal, require `"experimentalDecorators": true`

### 3. Closures & Scope

```javascript
### 3. Closures & Scope

**Closure Explanation:**
A closure is a function that has access to variables from its outer (enclosing) function's scope, even after that function has finished executing. This is fundamental to JavaScript and creates data privacy patterns.

**Why Closures Matter:**
- Data encapsulation - Keep state private
- Function factories - Create specialized functions
- Event handlers - Maintain context in callbacks
- Memoization - Cache expensive computations

```javascript
// Simple closure example
function createCounter() {
  let count = 0; // Private variable - only accessible through returned functions
  
  return {
    increment: () => ++count, // Function "closes over" count variable
    decrement: () => --count,
    getCount: () => count
  };
}

const counter = createCounter();
console.log(counter.increment()); // 1
console.log(counter.increment()); // 2
console.log(counter.getCount()); // 2

// The 'count' variable is private - can't access it directly
console.log(counter.count); // undefined - can't break encapsulation

// Why this is powerful: Data privacy pattern
// Without closures, you'd need to use objects with private fields or WeakMap

// Real-world example: API key management
function createApiClient(apiKey) {
  return {
    // apiKey is captured in closure - never exposed
    makeRequest: async (endpoint) => {
      return fetch(`https://api.example.com${endpoint}`, {
        headers: { 'Authorization': `Bearer ${apiKey}` }
      });
    }
  };
}

const client = createApiClient('secret-key-123');
// client.apiKey is undefined - key is protected by closure

// Another example: Function factory with closure
function createMultiplier(factor) {
  return (num) => num * factor; // 'factor' is closed over
}

const double = createMultiplier(2);
const triple = createMultiplier(3);

console.log(double(5)); // 10
console.log(triple(5)); // 15

// Common pitfall: Closures in loops
// This creates a closure issue
for (var i = 0; i < 3; i++) {
  setTimeout(() => {
    console.log(i); // Logs 3, 3, 3 (i is shared across all closures)
  }, 100);
}

// Fix 1: Use let (block scope)
for (let i = 0; i < 3; i++) {
  setTimeout(() => {
    console.log(i); // Logs 0, 1, 2 (each i is independent)
  }, 100);
}

// Fix 2: IIFE (Immediately Invoked Function Expression) for closure
for (var i = 0; i < 3; i++) {
  (function(j) {
    setTimeout(() => {
      console.log(j); // Logs 0, 1, 2
    }, 100);
  })(i); // Pass i to create independent closure
}
```

**Theory Notes:**
- Closures are automatically created in JavaScript - every function is a closure
- Inner functions have access to outer function variables (even after outer function returns)
- Each call to outer function creates new closure with independent variables
- Use closures for: data privacy, event handlers, callbacks, currying
- Be aware of closure memory implications - referenced variables aren't garbage collected

