## Database Integration

**Database Integration Explanation:**
Node.js applications need to interact with databases for persistent data storage. There are many libraries and patterns for working with different database types.

**Types of Databases:**
- **SQL (Relational)** - PostgreSQL, MySQL, SQLite (structured, ACID transactions)
- **NoSQL (Document)** - MongoDB, CouchDB (flexible schema, horizontal scaling)
- **Key-Value** - Redis, Memcached (fast caching, session storage)

### 1. SQL Databases (PostgreSQL with Prisma)

**Prisma Explanation:**
Prisma is a modern ORM (Object-Relational Mapping) that provides type-safe database access with auto-generated migrations. It abstracts database queries into a clean API.

**Why Prisma:**
- Type safety - Works with TypeScript for compile-time safety
- Auto-migrations - Manage schema changes
- Intuitive API - Readable, chainable queries
- Works with PostgreSQL, MySQL, SQLite, SQL Server

```javascript
// prisma/schema.prisma - Define data model
model User {
  id    Int     @id @default(autoincrement())
  name  String
  email String  @unique
  posts Post[]  // Relation to posts
}

model Post {
  id    Int     @id @default(autoincrement())
  title String
  user  User    @relation(fields: [userId], references: [id])
  userId Int
}

// Usage in Node.js
const { PrismaClient } = require('@prisma/client');
const prisma = new PrismaClient();

// CREATE - Insert new record
const user = await prisma.user.create({
  data: {
    name: 'John',
    email: 'john@example.com'
  }
});

// READ - Retrieve records
const users = await prisma.user.findMany(); // All users
const user = await prisma.user.findUnique({ where: { id: 1 } }); // By ID
const user = await prisma.user.findFirst({ where: { email: 'john@example.com' } }); // By condition

// UPDATE - Modify record
await prisma.user.update({
  where: { id: 1 },
  data: { name: 'Jane' }
});

// DELETE - Remove record
await prisma.user.delete({ where: { id: 1 } });

// Relations - Query with related data
const userWithPosts = await prisma.user.findUnique({
  where: { id: 1 },
  include: { posts: true } // Include all related posts
});

// Filtering and sorting
const activeUsers = await prisma.user.findMany({
  where: { active: true },
  orderBy: { name: 'asc' },
  take: 10 // Limit to 10
});
```

**Theory Notes:**
- ORM maps database tables to JavaScript objects
- Type safety prevents runtime database errors
- Relations enforce referential integrity
- Migrations track schema changes over time
- Connection pooling automatically managed

### 2. NoSQL (MongoDB with Mongoose)

**Mongoose Explanation:**
Mongoose is an ODM (Object Document Mapper) for MongoDB. It provides schema validation, middleware hooks, and a rich query API for MongoDB.

**Why Mongoose:**
- Schema validation - Define data structure even though MongoDB is schemaless
- Middleware hooks - Execute code before/after database operations
- Virtuals - Computed properties not stored in database
- Easier than raw MongoDB driver

```javascript
// Schema definition
const userSchema = new mongoose.Schema({
  name: { type: String, required: true },
  email: { type: String, required: true, unique: true },
  age: { type: Number, min: 0, max: 150 },
  createdAt: { type: Date, default: Date.now }
});

// Middleware - Execute before save
userSchema.pre('save', async function(next) {
  this.password = await bcrypt.hash(this.password, 10);
  next();
});

// Methods - Custom instance methods
userSchema.methods.getProfile = function() {
  return { name: this.name, email: this.email };
};

const User = mongoose.model('User', userSchema);

// CRUD operations
const user = new User({ name: 'John', email: 'john@example.com' });
await user.save(); // INSERT

const users = await User.find(); // SELECT all
const user = await User.findById(id); // SELECT by ID
await User.updateOne({ _id: id }, { name: 'Jane' }); // UPDATE
await User.deleteOne({ _id: id }); // DELETE

// Query helpers
const activeUsers = await User.find({ status: 'active' }).limit(10).sort({ name: 1 });
const count = await User.countDocuments();

// Aggregation pipeline - Complex queries
const stats = await User.aggregate([
  { $match: { status: 'active' } },
  { $group: { _id: '$department', count: { $sum: 1 } } }
]);
```

**Theory Notes:**
- Mongoose adds structure to flexible MongoDB
- Middleware (pre/post hooks) enable validation and side effects
- Virtuals allow computed properties without storage overhead
- Aggregation pipeline is MongoDB's SQL-like query language

### 3. Redis Caching

**Redis Explanation:**
Redis is an in-memory data store used for caching, sessions, and real-time data. It's extremely fast because data is stored in RAM.

**Why Redis:**
- Ultra-fast - In-memory access (microseconds vs milliseconds)
- Flexible data structures - Strings, lists, sets, hashes
- Expiration (TTL) - Automatic key removal
- Atomic operations - Thread-safe operations

```javascript
const redis = require('redis');
const client = redis.createClient();

// String operations - Key-value pairs
await client.set('key', 'value');
const value = await client.get('key');
await client.del('key'); // Delete key

// Expiration - Auto-delete after time
await client.setex('token', 3600, 'token_value'); // 1 hour TTL

// Hash operations - Object-like structure
await client.hset('user:1', 'name', 'John', 'email', 'john@example.com');
const userData = await client.hgetall('user:1');

// List operations - Queue-like structure
await client.rpush('queue', 'job1', 'job2'); // Push to end
const job = await client.lpop('queue'); // Pop from start

// Set operations - Unique values
await client.sadd('tags', 'nodejs', 'javascript');
const tags = await client.smembers('tags');

// Caching pattern - Check cache before database
async function getUserWithCache(userId) {
  const cached = await client.get(`user:${userId}`);
  if (cached) return JSON.parse(cached);
  
  const user = await User.findById(userId);
  await client.setex(`user:${userId}`, 3600, JSON.stringify(user));
  return user;
}

// Cache invalidation - Update when data changes
async function updateUser(userId, data) {
  const user = await User.findByIdAndUpdate(userId, data);
  await client.del(`user:${userId}`); // Invalidate cache
  return user;
}
```

**Theory Notes:**
- Redis is perfect for caching hot data
- Use TTL to prevent stale data
- Implement cache invalidation on updates
- Lists are perfect for queues
- Be aware of memory limits - everything in RAM

